No of outcome for a die = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 }

According to the question,

A = { 4, 5, 6 }

$\therefore$ P(A) = $\frac{3}{6}$

B = { 1, 2, 3, 4 }

$\therefore$ P(A) = $\frac{4}{6}$

A $\cap$ B = { 4 }

So P(A $\cap$ B) = $\frac{1}{6}$

We know, $P$\left( {A $\cup$ B} \right)  = $P$\left( A \right)  + $P$\left( B \right)  - $P$\left( {A \cap B} \right) 

$\therefore P$\left( {A $\cup$ B} \right)  = $\frac{3}{6} + \frac{4}{6} - \frac{1}{6} = {{7 - 1} \over 6} = \frac{6}{6}$ = 1

$\therefore$ Option (C) is correct.

